title: Every YAML file we write starts with ---, sops writes none
updated: 2026-08-20T05:46:14Z
---

Measured, sops 3.13.3, HEAD ee16750.

YAML::XS::Dump always emits a leading document-start marker, so *every* YAML
file this distribution writes carries one on line 1 -- the encrypted document
from encrypt/encrypt_file/encrypt_in_place/rotate/edit, and the plaintext from
decrypt_file. sops writes neither:

    our encrypt_file    -> "---\nhalf: ENC[...]"
    sops -e             -> "whole: ENC[...]"
    our decrypt_file    -> "---\nhalf: 1.5"
    sops -d             -> "whole: 2"

Benign as far as anything can tell: YAML resolves a document with or without
the marker identically, sops -d accepts our files (t/04-interop.t, 31/31), and
the MAC covers values, not the serialized text. So this is a cosmetic
divergence, not a compatibility one.

What makes it a ticket rather than a shrug is that it is nowhere in the POD.
lib/File/SOPS.pm and lib/File/SOPS/Format/YAML.pm both describe what a written
file looks like in detail -- "How a file is written", the emit() POD, the
decrypt_file output paragraph -- and none of them mentions that line 1 is not
what the reference implementation writes. A caller diffing our output against
sops -d, or feeding it to something stricter than a YAML parser, meets it with
no warning.

Two possible answers, and picking between them is the work:

  - document it (one sentence, cheapest, and consistent with how the other
    deliberate divergences in this distribution are handled), or
  - stop emitting it. That is a Format/YAML.pm emitter change, which is
    file-sops-wire territory rather than file-sops-format, because the encrypt
    side of the MAC depends on the emitter -- see ADR 0001. Also needs a check
    of whether the marker matters for the multi-document model in karr #31,
    where a leading separator is already dropped on parse.

Found while adding the ADR 0009 cross-reference to decrypt_file's POD; not
touched there, out of scope.
